# A pool's destroy another thread keeps pace with where no copy reaches the pool:
# the root makes a pool and a table in it, derives two frames into the second thread's table below a root it then moves into that table,
# and revokes below the pool's memory; once the capabilities to the pool are gone, the destroy deletes the root a step at a time,
# and between each two steps the second deletes the child that became a root and copies the one still below into its slot;
# so refusing copies of capabilities to a dying pool's objects would not end it; see DESIGN.md, "What a holder gave, it takes back".
